# uSD card prep — one card per drone — 2026-09-14
#
# Both drones need their OWN uSD card, formatted FAT32, with
# flying_drone_stack/tools/usd_thesis_config.txt copied to the card root AS config.txt
# (exact filename, not the original name -- the firmware only reads "config.txt").
# Do this once per card; cards then just live in each drone permanently.

# ── 1. Find the card's device node (do this AFTER inserting ONE card at a time --
#      makes it unambiguous which device just appeared) ─────────────────────────────────
lsblk
# Look for a new device (e.g. /dev/sdb1 or /dev/mmcblk0p1) that wasn't there before.
# DOUBLE CHECK this is the SD card and not a system disk before the next steps --
# mkfs on the wrong device destroys data with no warning.

# ── 2. Format FAT32 (replace sdX1 with the real device from step 1) ─────────────────────
sudo umount /dev/sdX1 2>/dev/null   # unmount first if auto-mounted
sudo mkfs.vfat -F 32 -n THESIS /dev/sdX1

# ── 3. Mount, copy config, unmount cleanly ───────────────────────────────────────────────
mkdir -p /tmp/sdcard_mount
sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /tmp/sdcard_mount
cp /home/flyingrobots/georg/flying_robot_course/flying_drone_stack/tools/usd_thesis_config.txt \
   /tmp/sdcard_mount/config.txt
sync
sudo umount /tmp/sdcard_mount

# ── 4. Repeat steps 1-3 for the SECOND card (cf_second's) ────────────────────────────────
# Insert the second card, run `lsblk` again to find its (likely different) device node,
# then repeat the format + copy for it too. Two separate physical cards, same config.txt
# content on both -- the config just says WHAT to log, not which drone it's on.

# ── 5. Insert each card into its drone, power on, confirm detection ─────────────────────
# cf231_active is on the DEV machine's check_usd_deck.py (URI radio://0/80/2M/E7E7E7E7E7) --
# copy that script's URI or just edit it in place for cf_second's URI
# (radio://0/80/2M/E7E7E7E7E9) to check that one too. Run from terminal 2 (sourced env):
python3 /home/flyingrobots/georg/flying_robot_course/flying_drone_stack/tools/check_usd_deck.py
# Expect: "usd param group found: [...]" and "usd.logging current value = ..." with no
# MISSING lines. If MISSING, the deck itself isn't seated/detected -- reseat it, don't
# just re-copy the config file.

# ── After both cards are prepped and confirmed detected ──────────────────────────────────
# Re-fly A8 (same command as before) -- run_formation.py already turns usd.logging on/off
# automatically around the scenario (`allcfs.setParam('usd.logging', 1/0)`), no extra flag
# needed. After landing, pull each card's binary log file off (card root, likely named by
# timestamp or index depending on firmware log-writer naming) and I'll write the
# uSD-vs-radio sync comparison once we have both files -- tools/merge_usd_logs.py and
# tools/decode_usd_log.py already exist for this, just never run on real 2-drone data yet.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# controller=7 (NA-INDI) single-drone hover test -- ONLY IF the full-INDI A8 gate fails
# tomorrow. Do NOT run any of this unless our own INDI genuinely fails its 2-drone A8 check.
# Written out in full now, NOT applied to the live yaml, so it's a fast copy-paste once the
# gate result is known -- not something to work out live under time pressure.
#
# Why this is safe by construction, not just "should be fine":
#   - flight.py's ramp phases (takeoff/landing) are HARDCODED to stabilizer.controller=6
#     (_RAMP_CONTROLLER, flight.py line ~274) regardless of what the yaml says. Only the
#     TRAJECTORY/hover phase reads the yaml's controller value. So cf231_active always takes
#     off and lands under the well-flown geometric controller; it only runs controller=7
#     during the level hover itself, after it's already stably airborne.
#   - This is the EXACT same mechanism already proven tonight for switching ctrl_mode between
#     phases (takeoff=0, trajectory=yaml value, landing=0) -- controller=7 just rides the same
#     "yaml_controller != _RAMP_CONTROLLER" check that already triggers the phase-switch apply()
#     call. No flight.py code changes needed; this was checked line by line (2026-09-14).
#   - Per-robot overrides are NOT used for this test (unlike cf_second's permanent pin) --
#     instead the *shared* `all:` controller value is changed, because this is a genuinely
#     single-drone, isolated test (cf_second disabled), so there's no other robot to protect
#     from the change. A per-robot override would ALSO apply during takeoff/landing (wrong --
#     you'd want controller=7 in the air, not on the ramp), so don't use one here.
#
# ── Step 0 (prerequisite, part of testing full INDI anyway, not controller=7-specific) ──────
# Remove cf231_active's temporary ctrl_mode:0 / pos_gains override in crazyflies.yaml (the one
# marked "REMOVE this override before flying step 2") so it falls back to the shared full-INDI
# default -- this has to happen before the full-INDI A8 attempt regardless of whether it passes.
#
# ── Step 1: flash controller=7 onto cf231_active (skip if already done) ─────────────────────
cd /home/flyingrobots/georg/flying_robot_course/flying_drone_stack/firmware_app
make DRONE=bl cload
# Confirm the build banner says "Build for the cf21bl platform!" before flashing (2026-09-13
# lesson -- wrong platform flashed once before). Confirmed buildable from a clean checkout on
# 2026-09-14 (dev machine): 38.5KB RAM free, no code changes needed.

# ── Step 2: isolate cf231_active alone (mirror of the cf_second isolation done earlier today) ─
# In crazyswarm2/crazyflie/config/crazyflies.yaml:
#   cf_second: enabled: true -> false
#   all: firmware_params: stabilizer: controller: 6 -> 7
# Restart terminal 1's `ros2 launch crazyflie launch.py` after saving.

# ── Step 3: single-drone hover, tethered if available ────────────────────────────────────────
ros2 run crazyflie_examples flight -- --trajectory hover --duration 15 --height 0.6
# Start LOW (0.6m) and short -- this is a first flight of a brand-new, numerically-verified-only
# controller. Do not go straight to 1.4m or a long duration. If clean, a second run at a more
# normal height is reasonable; still hover-only, still not A8, still not 2-drone.

# ── Step 4: revert afterwards, whatever the outcome ──────────────────────────────────────────
#   all: firmware_params: stabilizer: controller: 7 -> 6
#   cf_second: enabled: false -> true
# Restart terminal 1's launch again before any further flight.
